        <div id="batteries" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Rechargeable, AAA or Hybrid: The Battery Decision
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most important choice in a headlamp is how it is powered, and there are three options. Built-in rechargeable headlamps, like the Nitecore NU25 UL and BioLite 425, are light and convenient and charge over USB-C. AAA headlamps, like the Black Diamond Spot 400, run on batteries you can buy anywhere and store for years. Hybrid headlamps, like the Petzl ACTIK CORE, take a rechargeable pack or AAA batteries in the same compartment.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyday use, rechargeable wins. You charge it from the same cable as your phone and never buy batteries. For an emergency kit, the question is what happens on day three of an outage when your power bank is empty. A built-in battery headlamp is dark until you find power. A hybrid or AAA headlamp keeps going on a pack of batteries from the drawer.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">At least one headlamp in the house should run on AAA batteries.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Rechargeable is better day to day. AAA is better on day three. A hybrid like the Petzl ACTIK CORE gives you both in one lamp.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Larger headlamps like the Fenix HM65R use a removable 18650 lithium-ion cell. That holds much more energy than a built-in pack or three AAAs, which is why it can deliver far more light for longer. You can also keep a spare charged 18650 in the kit and swap it in seconds, which doubles runtime without needing any power at all.
          </p>

