        <div id="lumens" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Many Lumens a Headlamp Really Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because a headlamp lights what is a metre or two in front of you, it needs far less output than a flashlight. Reading, cooking and fixing things up close is comfortable at 50 to 150 lumens. Walking around a dark house or garden is fine at 100 to 300. Only searching at distance, working outside in rain or moving fast over rough ground calls for more than 400.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the 400-lumen headlamps on this list are genuinely enough for most people, and why the Fenix's 1,400 lumens are aimed at outdoor work. On a headlamp, very high output also means bouncing glare off anything close to you and blinding anyone you turn to face. Most of the time you will use the lowest mode that does the job, and the battery will last much longer for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Like flashlights, headlamps are rated under the ANSI FL1 standard, so the peak lumen figure is a short burst with fresh batteries. Most headlamps step down from their top mode to protect the LED and battery. Compare models on their medium-mode runtime in the manufacturer's chart, not the headline lumens, because medium is where you will spend the evening.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rated Peak Output</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1,400</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Fenix HM65R</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">600</p>
