                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Black Diamond Spot 400 / Nitecore NU25 UL</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you are torn between two headlamps, the better low mode and the better battery matter more than the bigger number. A headlamp that runs all night on low is more useful in an outage than one that runs for an hour on high.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="red-light" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Red Light, Beam Shape and Modes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every good headlamp now has a red light mode, and it is more useful in an outage than most people expect. Red light preserves your eyes' dark adaptation, so you can still see by moonlight when you switch it off. It also does not dazzle the person you are talking to, which matters when everyone is wearing a headlamp around a kitchen table or you are checking on a sleeping child.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beam shape is the next thing to look at. A flood beam spreads light widely and evenly, which is what you want for cooking, reading and close work. A spot beam throws light further for walking and searching. The best headlamps, including every one on this list, offer both, and some, like the Fenix HM65R and Nitecore NU25 UL, let you run them together.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then look at how the modes are arranged. A headlamp you use a few times a year should come on in a sensible low or medium mode with one press, not blast you with full power or strobe. The Black Diamond Spot 400's PowerTap, where a tap on the side jumps between full and dimmed, is a good example of a feature that makes a headlamp easier to use, not harder.
          </p>

          <p>
            A lock mode is worth having in a kit headlamp. Without it, a headlamp in a drawer or bag can be switched on by something pressing the button, and you discover a flat battery exactly when you need it. Most modern headlamps lock with a long press or a twist, and it is worth learning when the lamp arrives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="comfort" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Fit and Comfort for Hours of Wear
          </h2>

          <p>
            In an outage you may wear a headlamp for hours at a time, so comfort matters more than it does for a quick trip to the garage. The two things that make a headlamp uncomfortable are weight at the front, which tips the lamp down and pulls on the band, and a narrow strap that presses into the head.
          </p>

          <p>
            The BioLite HeadLamp 425 solves the first problem by moving the battery to the back of the band so the weight balances. The Nitecore NU25 UL solves it by being so light that balance hardly matters. The Fenix HM65R, with its 18650 cell up front, is the heaviest here and is best worn for working rather than all evening.
          </p>

          <p>
            Band width and material matter too. A wide, soft band spreads the pressure. A shock-cord band, like the Nitecore's, is lighter but can feel tight and less secure on a long day. If you wear glasses or a hat, try the headlamp with them, because some lamps sit awkwardly over a peak or press frames into your temples.
          </p>

          <p>
            Children need their own headlamp, sized for a smaller head and with a simple interface. A headlamp makes an outage far less frightening for a child, because they control their own light. Buy something cheap, rechargeable and easy to switch on and off, and keep it with the rest of the kit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outage-plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. A Headlamp Plan for Power Outages
          </h2>

          <p>
            The simplest plan is one headlamp per person, kept in the same place as the rest of the outage supplies, and charged at the start of each storm season. Make at least one of them a hybrid or AAA model, and keep a sealed pack of AAA batteries with it. That covers the scenario where an outage outlasts every rechargeable in the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Charge rechargeable headlamps first thing in the morning during an outage, from a <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">power bank</a> or power station, so they are full by dusk. A small power bank holds many times the energy of a headlamp battery, so one bank can keep several headlamps topped up for days. Do not leave charging until it is dark and everyone needs their lamp.
          </p>

