        <div id="weather" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Water Resistance and Durability
          </h2>

          <p>
            Outages often come with storms, so a headlamp that might be used outside in rain needs a real water rating. IPX4 means it survives splashing and rain. IPX7 and IPX8 mean it survives being dropped in water. The Black Diamond Spot 400 is rated IPX8 and the Fenix HM65R IP68, which covers anything short of deliberate diving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Durability matters for a lamp that might be dropped on concrete in a basement or knocked against a tree. Metal-bodied headlamps like the Fenix shrug off knocks. Plastic headlamps are lighter and mostly fine, but check that the battery door closes firmly and that the hinge between lamp and bracket is solid, because those are the parts that fail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold matters too. Lithium-ion and lithium AAA cells perform better in the cold than alkaline AAA cells, which lose much of their capacity below freezing. If your outages tend to be winter ice storms, run AAA headlamps on lithium AAA cells for the kit, or keep the headlamp in an inside pocket until you need it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep it clean and dry between uses. Wipe the lens, let the band dry if it gets wet, and store it with the battery charged or the batteries removed. A headlamp left with corroding alkaline cells in it for a year is a common way to find a dead light when you need it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Battery Care and Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rechargeable headlamps last longest when charged every few months rather than left full and hot or flat for a long time. A good routine is to charge every headlamp in the house at the start of storm season and again at the start of winter, and to use them now and then in between.
          </p>

          <p>
            For AAA headlamps in a kit, either store the batteries outside the lamp or use lithium AAA cells, which resist leaking far better than alkalines and hold their charge for years. Write the date on the pack. If you use rechargeable AAA cells, choose low self-discharge types so they are still mostly full when you need them.
          </p>

          <p>
            For headlamps with a removable 18650, use the cell the maker supplies or a reputable replacement of the same type, and carry spares in a plastic case away from keys and coins. A short circuit across loose cells can heat them quickly, and that risk is easy to avoid.
          </p>

          <p>
            Built-in batteries cannot be replaced, so treat those headlamps as having a finite life of a few years of regular use. That is fine for a light, comfortable everyday lamp. It is the reason the kit's backup should be a headlamp with a battery you can change.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Headlamp Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For one headlamp that does everything, buy the Petzl ACTIK CORE. It charges like a phone, runs on AAA batteries when it has to, and gives enough light for every indoor job and most outdoor ones. It is the safest single choice for a household.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you will be working outside after storms, clearing branches or running a generator, add the Fenix HM65R. Its separate spot and flood, removable 18650 and metal body make it the most capable lamp here.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a cheap, simple spare that works from stored batteries, the Black Diamond Spot 400 is hard to beat. And if comfort over a long evening matters most, choose the BioLite HeadLamp 425 or the Nitecore NU25 UL, and keep a power bank to top them up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, buy one per adult, keep them with the outage supplies and charge them before storm season. A headlamp you can find in the dark is worth more than any feature on the spec sheet.
          </p>
        </div>

